1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,400
After a crash, the first real job is documentation. Which cars,
which damage, which road, and what actually happened. ClaimScene
turns accident photos into a case a claims handler can trust.
Honest, illustrated, and never faked.

2
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:31,833
That matters now, because generative video made accident scenes
easy to fake and hard to trust. A convincing AI reconstruction
that is really just a guess is dangerous as evidence. Insurers
and courts are already warning about synthetic media in claims.

3
00:00:31,833 --> 00:00:48,300
Every case starts with photos, yours or a sample scenario like
this one. A vision model reads them into a closed vocabulary
only: vehicle kind, colour, approach, and a twelve position
clock for damage and impact, never a free coordinate.

4
00:00:48,300 --> 00:01:03,700
The proposed scene is yours to correct. Drag a vehicle between
the four approaches, or drag its handle around the twelve clock
positions, and the schematic redraws live. And the app is blunt:
confirming a field is not the same as verifying it.

5
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:17,133
Confirm, and rendering runs as a background job the app submits
and polls, since a real illustration render can take minutes.
First the deterministic schematic, then the disclosed AI clip,
then the sealed manifest.

6
00:01:17,133 --> 00:01:28,200
Both layers sit sealed, side by side. The schematic on the left
is the reconstruction, drawn by code. The clip on the right
carries visual style only, never the facts.

7
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:44,667
Underneath, it is ports and adapters. A vision ladder reads the
photos, code draws the schematic to the same bytes every run,
Backblaze B2 holds the evidence, and Genblaze animates our own
schematic raster, never a generated still.

8
00:01:44,667 --> 00:01:58,700
Storage is Backblaze B2, and it is content addressed. Every
input photo, every artifact and the manifest lands under a key
that is its own hash, and the seal records where each photo came
from and under what license.

9
00:01:58,700 --> 00:02:09,533
The sealed manifest states that split in plain language.
Repeated live renders proved the model does not reliably keep
the layout it is seeded with, so nothing provable is left to it.

10
00:02:09,533 --> 00:02:20,067
Press Verify, and the SHA-256 seal recomputes right in your
browser. Behind it sits a receipt of eighteen named checks, each
re-run from the stored bytes on the server.

11
00:02:20,067 --> 00:02:32,600
And the accuracy is measured, not asserted. The primary model
scores one hundred percent weighted field accuracy on a
committed seven scenario evaluation set, with a dated scoreboard
living in the repo.

12
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:43,900
Over eight hundred automated tests, secret scanning and a scored
readiness gate run in CI on every change. And the live health
endpoint names the exact backends that actually served you.

13
00:02:43,900 --> 00:02:56,200
ClaimScene is live on Cloud Run, built on Genblaze and Backblaze
B2, and the whole project is open source. Honest accident
documentation. Illustrated, and never faked.
